## 9. BACKTESTING STANDARDS

### 9.1 Validation Requirements

Before any system goes live with real money, these metrics must be achieved:

```
Minimum acceptable backtest results:
Win rate:        > 52%       (anything below = coin flip)
Profit factor:   > 1.5       (gross wins / gross losses)
Sharpe ratio:    > 1.0       (risk-adjusted return)
Max drawdown:    < 20%       (survivable)
Total trades:    > 50        (statistical significance)
Backtest period: > 5 years   (must include bull + bear cycles)
```

### 9.2 Walk-Forward Validation

```
Standard backtest → just historical performance
Walk-forward      → simulates real-time performance

Process:
1. Train on 2 years of data
2. Test on next 6 months (out-of-sample)
3. Roll forward 6 months
4. Retrain, test again
5. Repeat across all available history

If walk-forward significantly underperforms backtest:
→ System is overfit
→ Do NOT deploy with real money
```

### 9.3 The 70% Rule

```
If your backtest shows > 70% win rate:
SOMETHING IS WRONG WITH YOUR DATA.

Either:
- Lookahead bias (future data leaked into training)
- Overfitting (memorising history, not learning patterns)
- Survivorship bias (cherry-picked periods)
- Transaction costs ignored

Realistic win rate with a good system: 55–65%
Anything higher should be treated with extreme suspicion.
```

