## 15. FEATURE ENGINEERING PRINCIPLES

These principles prevent the most common ML mistakes in trading:

```
PRINCIPLE 1: NO LOOKAHEAD BIAS
Every feature must use ONLY past data at the time of prediction.
Using df['Close'].shift(-5) anywhere in features = data leakage.
Using tomorrow's news to predict today = cheating.
Test: Can this feature be computed at 9 AM for same-day signal? Yes → safe.

PRINCIPLE 2: NORMALISATION
All features must be bounded before entering ML.
Unbounded features (raw price, raw volume) destabilise XGBoost.
Target range: -1.0 to +1.0 or 0.0 to 1.0.
Apply winsorisation (clip at 2-3 sigma) before scaling.

PRINCIPLE 3: CORRELATION MANAGEMENT
Do not add two features that measure the same thing.
RSI and StochRSI are correlated — include both anyway because
they respond differently in trending vs ranging markets.
But RSI-14 and RSI-21 should not both be included.
Remove any pair with Pearson correlation > 0.85.

PRINCIPLE 4: MISSING DATA
FinBERT returns 0.0 if no news articles (not NaN).
yfinance returns NaN on holidays — forward-fill.
Missing fundamentals → use most recent quarter's value.
Never pass NaN to XGBoost — it will error or produce garbage.

PRINCIPLE 5: FEATURE COUNT vs SAMPLE COUNT
Rule of thumb: need 50+ samples per feature for reliable learning.
HDFC Bank daily data: 6,300 rows.
83 swing features: 6300/83 = 76 samples per feature. Acceptable.
Adding 200 features to 6,300 rows = overfitting guaranteed.
```

